---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import allSources from "../data/sources.json";
import searched from "../data/searched.json";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

/* This table was a hand-typed list of ten for months, while the archive went
   on using a hundred and forty-eight more. It is now driven by the source
   register itself, so it cannot fall behind again. */
const byKind = {};
for (const x of allSources) (byKind[x.kind || "Other"] ||= []).push(x);
const kinds = Object.entries(byKind).sort((a, b) => b[1].length - a[1].length);
const host = (url) => { try { return new URL(url).host.replace(/^www\./, ""); } catch { return ""; } };
---
<Base title="About" eyebrow="Method, ethics and sources"
      dek="How this archive decides what it is willing to say.">
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The confidence system</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A claim is never stored as a fact. It is stored as an assertion with a source and a confidence, and the interface always shows which. Three states, on every date, every relationship and every claim about character.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Documented</span>
      <p class="ab">A named source with a folio reference and, wherever possible, the scan itself.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span>
      <p class="ab">A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so it can be challenged and overturned.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip lore">Family lore</span>
      <p class="ab">Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious and the family said it; labelled because pretending otherwise is how family myths become family history.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<Rule name="arrows" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Living people</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Data protection law reaches the living and not the dead, and a private tree is exempt from it while a public website is not. So the rule is structural rather than editorial: anyone marked living is <strong>omitted from the generated site at build time</strong> — not hidden behind CSS, not behind a login, not present in the data with a flag. Not rendered at all.</p>
    <p>A living person appears only if they have asked to. A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<Rule name="pomegranate" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Successes and failings both</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Illegitimacy, poverty, desertion, debt, drink, crime, children who did not survive — these are in the registers and they are the truth of most families. The policy: the closer to living memory, the higher the bar. Beyond about 1920, nothing without a named source. Within living memory, nothing about anyone without their family's consent.</p>
    <p>Hardship is told in its context. Camillo De Franceschi went to prison for two years at twenty-one; that is not a stain to be managed but the most interesting fact about him, and it belongs on his page in full.</p>
    <p>These places sit on the fault line of one of Europe's most contested memories. Between a quarter and a third of a million people left Istria, Kvarner and Dalmatia after 1943, and there are two national narratives that do not agree about any of it. This archive's position: <strong>report what happened to our people, with sources; use both toponyms always; do not adjudicate between national grievances.</strong> Some of this family stayed and some left, and both are owed the same dignity.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<Rule name="helm" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">
    <strong>{allSources.length} sources</strong>, grouped by what kind of thing they are.
    The <a href={u("/searched/")}>search register</a> records every one of the
    <strong>{searched.rows.length} searches</strong> made against them, including the ones that found nothing.
  </p>
  <div style="margin-top:22px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:22px">
    {kinds.map(([kind, list]) => (
      <div>
        <div class="lab">{kind} — {list.length}</div>
        <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:8px">
          <table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Where</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{list.map(x => (
            <tr>
              <td>{x.url ? <a href={x.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{x.title}</a> : x.title}</td>
              <td class="m">{host(x.url) || x.where || ""}</td>
              <td>{(x.what || "").split(/(?<=\.)\s/)[0]}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}</tbody></table>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">Nothing here yet establishes descent. The register holdings are documented; the lines above them are not. That is the work.</div>
</section>
</Base>
